# The projection problem

A literate-program author edits pieces of code inside an explanation. A programming-
language tool expects complete source files. Both views are legitimate, but they do
not have the same shape.

A fragment may be defined far from the output that uses it. One fragment may be
inserted several times. A reference may add indentation. A source template may wrap
the assembled program in text that does not appear in any fragment. If a language
server reports that a name occupies a certain range in the assembled file, the
editor must decide whether that range corresponds to text the author can actually
see and edit.

Literate solves this with a **projection**: an in-memory assembled source file plus
records that connect unchanged pieces of that file to their exact origins in the
book. A projection is not an approximate preview and not a saved generated file. It
uses the same fragment order, expansion rules, escaping, templates, and validation
policy as generation, while retaining provenance that ordinary generated source
would lose.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Snapshot[Captured literate project] --> Validate[Validate the complete fragment graph]
    Validate --> Expand[Assemble every source output in memory]
    Expand --> Wrap[Apply source templates]
    Wrap --> Projection[Projected files and literal mappings]
    Projection --> Server[Language server]
    Server --> Reply[Generated-file ranges]
    Reply --> Map[Map safe ranges back to the book]
```

The downward arrows show a request moving from the book toward the language server;
the final arrows show a reply returning through the same captured projection. The
diagram omits editor scheduling and protocol details. Its important point is that
mapping is not a later search through similar text. It is evidence collected while
the source is assembled.

## Projection must agree with generation

A language server is useful only if it sees the program that generation would
produce from the same snapshot. Literate therefore projects all source outputs as
one atomic operation. A missing fragment, cycle, invalid reference operator, missing
template, destination conflict, or exceeded expansion bound prevents every projected
file from being returned.

Validation includes fragment families that no output currently reaches. This may
seem stricter than a request at one cursor needs, but it preserves one project
meaning. Interactive language service must not quietly accept a graph that normal
generation rejects.

Projection also bounds intermediate work. Repeated references can make a small graph
expand into a very large amount of text, and a template can contain the code slot
more than once. Literate counts copied text, mapping segments, and nesting while it
works. Crossing a limit reports failure rather than returning a partial projection.
A partial file could look plausible to a server while representing no valid program.

## A mapping records unchanged literal text

Each mapping joins one contiguous source interval to one equal-length generated
interval. If a source interval begins at `s` and its generated occurrence begins at
`g`, a position `p` inside that interval maps to
`g + (p - s)`. The subtraction measures how far the position lies from the start of
the source interval; adding the same distance to `g` finds the corresponding place
in generated text.

That simple arithmetic is trustworthy only for text copied without alteration.
Literate consequently leaves several kinds of text unmapped:

- prose and Markdown structure;
- fence headers and fragment reference markers;
- indentation inserted at a reference site;
- a slash removed while interpreting an escaped fragment opener;
- source-template text surrounding a code slot;
- any synthetic spacing created while Markdown interprets a container or tab.

The policy is deliberately conservative. An unmapped character does not mean that
the character is unimportant to the generated program. It means that Literate
cannot point to an equal, directly editable character in the captured source.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A[Literal source text] -->|equal text| B[Mapped generated segment]
    C[Reference marker] -->|expands to other text| D[No mapping]
    E[Inserted indentation] -->|created during assembly| F[No mapping]
    G[Template wrapper] -->|not fragment source| H[No mapping]
```

These arrows classify provenance, not control flow. The diagram intentionally omits
line and offset arithmetic. What matters is the safety rule: only unchanged literal
text receives a reversible connection.

## Lines are not as simple as they look

Markdown presents fence contents in a normalized form, while the editor still works
with the original document. A source may use a line feed, a carriage return followed
by a line feed, or a carriage return alone. Projected files normalize line endings,
but mappings retain offsets into the original text.

Literate handles literal content line by line. Newline pieces receive their own
mapping evidence, so a two-unit source line ending is never misrepresented as a
one-unit equal-length range. Empty lines and the end of an unfinished fence may
retain zero-width cursor anchors. Those anchors allow insertion at a real source
position without pretending that an absent character was copied.

Markdown containers create another subtle case. A quotation or list may consume
some leading indentation, including part of a tab. Literate maps only the unchanged
suffix that still appears literally in both representations. It does not map
virtual spaces invented by Markdown normalization.

## UTF-16 is a coordinate agreement

People often describe a position as a line and a character. Tools still need to
agree on what “character” means. JavaScript strings, VS Code, and the configured
language-server connection use UTF-16 code units. Literate therefore measures both
absolute offsets and line columns in UTF-16.

Most familiar characters occupy one code unit. Some symbols, including many emoji,
occupy two. In the text `😀name`, the `n` begins at UTF-16 column two even though a
reader sees one symbol before it. Counting visible symbols or Unicode code points
would move every later range on that line to the wrong place.

UTF-16 is not claimed to be the best measure of what a person sees. It is the shared
coordinate system at this boundary. A server that selects another position encoding
cannot safely participate, because a numerically valid range could identify the
wrong source text.

## One source interval may have several occurrences

A fragment reference can insert the same source text more than once. A template can
also contain several code slots. Each insertion receives a distinct mapping object,
even when the source interval and generated bytes are identical.

A request beginning in literate source chooses the first matching occurrence in
deterministic output and expansion order. That exact occurrence then travels with
the request. A returning range is accepted only if it lies inside the same mapping
object in the same projected file. Equal text elsewhere is not enough, and a newly
constructed copy of the mapping record is not treated as the original occurrence.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Source[One source interval] --> First[First generated occurrence]
    Source --> Second[Second generated occurrence]
    Cursor[Cursor in source] --> First
    First --> Reply[Reply bound to first occurrence]
    Reply --> Check{Still inside that occurrence?}
    Check -->|Yes| SourceRange[Return the source range]
    Check -->|No| Omit[Return no mapped range]
```

The arrows from the source show reuse; the cursor arrow shows deterministic request
selection. This model is sufficient for services that only observe code: duplicate
answers can be deduplicated, and an unsafe range can be omitted. A proposed edit is
more dangerous because changing one source interval affects its other expansions.
That stricter boundary is the subject of Chapter 22.

`projection position helpers` converts between UTF-16 positions and absolute
offsets without losing occurrence identity.

```ts : <<projection position helpers>>=
export function mapSourcePosition(files: LanguageProjection[], sourcePath: string, offset: number): SourceProjectionPosition | undefined {
    if (!Number.isInteger(offset) || offset < 0) { return undefined; }
    const path = safeRelativePath(sourcePath);
    for (const file of files) {
        for (const mapping of file.mappings) {
            if (mapping.sourcePath === path && offset >= mapping.sourceStart
                && (offset < mapping.sourceEnd || (offset === mapping.sourceEnd && mapping.endCursor))) {
                return { file, offset: mapping.generatedStart + offset - mapping.sourceStart, mapping };
            }
        }
    }
    return undefined;
}

export function mapGeneratedRange(file: LanguageProjection, start: number, end: number, mapping: ProjectionMapping): { start: number; end: number } | undefined {
    if (!file.mappings.includes(mapping) || !Number.isInteger(start) || !Number.isInteger(end)
        || start > end || start < mapping.generatedStart || end > mapping.generatedEnd
        || (start === end && start === mapping.generatedEnd && !mapping.endCursor)) { return undefined; }
    return { start: mapping.sourceStart + start - mapping.generatedStart, end: mapping.sourceStart + end - mapping.generatedStart };
}
```

## Seams stop reverse mapping

A server range may cross from literal text into inserted indentation, through a
fragment boundary, or into a template wrapper. Literate does not widen the range to
nearby source and does not search for matching words. Reverse mapping through one
request occurrence succeeds only inside that occurrence's literal segment.

Some read-only results can still keep their useful content when an optional range is
unsafe. Diagnostics follow a different rule: a large generated range may be split
into its separate mapped intersections, so each highlighted source range remains
honest. Navigation normally selects one deterministic contiguous source sequence.
The following chapters explain these policies in their own contexts.

A projection therefore carries two products together: complete source for the
language server and limited proof about where that source came from. The next
chapter adds the long-lived process that consumes those projected files without
requiring them to be written to disk.
